import{_ as a,o as t,c as r,a2 as n}from"./chunks/framework.Bx6eOp9J.js";const h=JSON.parse('{"title":"2026-10-28 Agent Research and Product Intelligence","description":"Durable takeover, viewer identity, recovery evidence and changes reaching official releases.","frontmatter":{"title":"2026-10-28 Agent Research and Product Intelligence","description":"Durable takeover, viewer identity, recovery evidence and changes reaching official releases."},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/runtime/daily-intelligence/2026-10-28.md","filePath":"en/runtime/daily-intelligence/2026-10-28.md","lastUpdated":1793158210000}'),s={name:"en/runtime/daily-intelligence/2026-10-28.md"};function i(o,e,l,c,d,p){return t(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="_2026-10-28-agent-research-and-product-intelligence" tabindex="-1">2026-10-28 Agent Research and Product Intelligence <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2026-10-28-agent-research-and-product-intelligence" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2026-10-28 Agent Research and Product Intelligence&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The key conclusion is that <strong>remote continuity must preserve the original execution identity, actor authority must not borrow host credentials, and recoverability must be separated from complete evidence</strong>. Paperclip and Paradev advance the first two boundaries on main. Yesterday&#39;s merged CrewAI security fixes, AG2 timeouts and Langfuse Government/FIPS changes now have explicit release inclusion.</p><p>Run date: 2026-10-28 Asia/Shanghai. Actual start: 11:18:02. The main incremental window begins at the previous successful check start, October 27 20:50:12, and ends at this start. Findings are deduplicated against the checkpoint and published October 27 report. Partial sources retain earlier pending windows; older PRs/papers are not relabeled as new. All CodeFlowMu/FCoP relationships are <strong>pending validation leads</strong>.</p><h2 id="_1-paperclip-merges-durable-remote-takeover-cross-artifact-qualification-remains-open" tabindex="-1">1. Paperclip merges durable remote takeover; cross-artifact qualification remains open <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-paperclip-merges-durable-remote-takeover-cross-artifact-qualification-remains-open" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1. Paperclip merges durable remote takeover; cross-artifact qualification remains open&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：MERGED #17097/#17439/#17455; OPEN #17456; RELEASE NOT VERIFIED</strong></p><p>#17097 adds optional external checkpoints for Runner control state. #17439 persists company/run-bound encrypted records in Postgres and adopts the original runner, session and active turn under a fenced lease. Committed tool receipts use current authority, reads are reauthorized and uncertain mutations are held; checkpoint pools are capped at two connections per database owner. #17455 merges immutable A/B artifact and phase-evidence qualification machinery, but actual A-to-B/B-to-A, drain-free Cloud rollout and the complete agent/viewer matrix remain open. Recoverable transcript delivery #17456 is OPEN, not delivered.</p><p>High learning value and competitive risk; retain the main radar with greater weight on remote continuity. Pending FCoP lead: distinguish HTTP readiness, ownership, authenticated attachment, resumed processing and settled backlog. Interaction: uncertain effects and retaining failed alongside passing qualification evidence.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/paperclipai/paperclip/pull/17097" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/paperclipai/paperclip/pull/17439" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a> · <a href="https://github.com/paperclipai/paperclip/pull/17455" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 3</a> · <a href="https://github.com/paperclipai/paperclip/pull/17456" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 4</a></p><h2 id="_2-superset-paradev-binds-pr-actions-to-the-viewer-instead-of-host-credentials" tabindex="-1">2. Superset / Paradev binds PR actions to the viewer instead of host credentials <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-superset-paradev-binds-pr-actions-to-the-viewer-instead-of-host-credentials" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2. Superset / Paradev binds PR actions to the viewer instead of host credentials&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：MERGED #8765/#8799/#8980; RELEASE NOT VERIFIED</strong></p><p>The final #8799 path chooses the viewer&#39;s local GitHub credentials, then that viewer&#39;s Paradev GitHub connection, otherwise requests connection. GitHub refusals do not trigger retries with another source. Tokens remain in the desktop main process, not the renderer. Host gh and sandbox creator/App-token fallbacks are removed; actions use per-viewer capabilities. #8980 extends the identity to diff-pane/Review-tab replies and resolution with shared caches. Manual in-app verification remains unchecked in the PR. Repository ID 1080370450 reconfirms the old-to-new mapping.</p><p>High learning value and competitive risk; increase identity-isolation attention on the main radar. Pending CodeFlowMu lead: actor, execution host and resource identity are distinct; credential fallback after refusal can expand authority. Interaction: local-versus-organization policy, audit attribution and cache behavior after revocation.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/ParadevAI/Paradev/pull/8765" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/ParadevAI/Paradev/pull/8799" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a> · <a href="https://github.com/ParadevAI/Paradev/pull/8980" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 3</a></p><h2 id="_3-codex-0-165-0-ships-interruption-safe-wakes-memory-defaults-and-marketplace-validation" tabindex="-1">3. Codex 0.165.0 ships interruption-safe wakes, memory defaults and marketplace validation <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-codex-0-165-0-ships-interruption-safe-wakes-memory-defaults-and-marketplace-validation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3. Codex 0.165.0 ships interruption-safe wakes, memory defaults and marketplace validation&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：RELEASED</strong></p><p>Released October 27 at 20:09 UTC. Queued agent messages no longer restart work after interruption or a switch to Plan. Dropping local response futures cancels remote MCP requests. Marketplace URL validation closes a discrepancy between the checked host and the host Git actually contacts. Fresh profiles default to memory v2 and external-agent memory import is supported. Attached app-server thread forks remain experimental. The release includes etraut-openai&#39;s direct fix for default approval-reviewer selection.</p><p>Pending lead: stop intent must constrain later wakes; imported memory requires separate provenance/user-boundary checks. A default does not establish isolation. Interaction: remote cancellation limits and durable message queues across Plan transitions.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/openai/codex/releases/tag/rust-v0.165.0" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a></p><h2 id="_4-codex-main-gates-state-changing-multi-agent-calls-through-guardian" tabindex="-1">4. Codex main gates state-changing multi-agent calls through Guardian <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-codex-main-gates-state-changing-multi-agent-calls-through-guardian" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;4. Codex main gates state-changing multi-agent calls through Guardian&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：MERGED #57451/#57522; STABLE INCLUSION NOT VERIFIED</strong></p><p>With GuardianNativeMultiAgentHistory enabled, #57451 requires review of wrapped Multi-Agent V2 spawn_agent, send_message, followup_task and interrupt_agent calls, includes them in asynchronous native-tool scoring and preserves namespace configuration. #57522 adds isolated reviews in temporary sessions without reading/changing reusable reviewers or carrying cached snapshots, preserving prewarmed sessions. These main merges are not listed in 0.165.0; same-day stable publication does not establish inclusion. The history flag was under development and disabled by default in yesterday&#39;s verified state.</p><p>Pending lead: spawn, communication, follow-up and interruption can alter authority/execution state; reviewers also need session-contamination boundaries. Interaction: isolated review versus shared history and behavior when multi-agent context is missing.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/openai/codex/pull/57451" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/openai/codex/pull/57522" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a></p><h2 id="_5-claude-code-2-1-312-bounds-hook-concurrency-and-removes-safety-event-telemetry" tabindex="-1">5. Claude Code 2.1.312 bounds hook concurrency and removes safety_event telemetry <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-claude-code-2-1-312-bounds-hook-concurrency-and-removes-safety-event-telemetry" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5. Claude Code 2.1.312 bounds hook concurrency and removes safety_event telemetry&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：RELEASED</strong></p><p>Version 2.1.312 fixes missing managed-policy refresh after VS Code sign-in, overwriting large instruction files after reading only their prefix, duplicate cloud execution after failed sends and outdated Code Review commits after late push events. Prompt/agent hooks are limited to 24 concurrent per session including subagents, plus at most 12 waiting beyond event timeouts; sub-second timeouts count as one second. The release explicitly stops sending the claude_code.safety_event OpenTelemetry event.</p><p>Pending lead: governance evidence cannot depend on one telemetry event; policy refresh, complete file context and hook resource limits need observability. Interaction: replacement evidence after safety_event removal, resources held by timed-out hooks and verification of the reviewed commit.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code/releases/tag/v2.1.312" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a></p><h2 id="_6-openhands-sdk-unifies-persisted-budgets-and-tolerates-damaged-event-history" tabindex="-1">6. OpenHands SDK unifies persisted budgets and tolerates damaged event history <a class="header-anchor" href="#_6-openhands-sdk-unifies-persisted-budgets-and-tolerates-damaged-event-history" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;6. OpenHands SDK unifies persisted budgets and tolerates damaged event history&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：MERGED #6257/#6279/#6197/#6259; RELEASE NOT VERIFIED</strong></p><p>#6279 follows #6257 by making ConversationState/base_state.json the single persisted owner of max_budget_per_run. Children inherit the current cap, forks copy it and full state snapshots replace rather than merge caches so cleared caps do not linger. Despite the per_run name, enforcement checks accumulated conversation cost between steps and can overshoot by one LLM call. #6197 loads surviving event files across gaps and truncates missing ancestry with warnings. #6259 protects timestamps/Literals from secret masking but does not repair already-corrupted history. Enyst directly contributes the budget follow-up.</p><p>Pending lead: expose budget ownership, accumulation scope and overshoot; readable damaged logs are not complete audit traces. Interaction: propagate missing-history evidence to verifiers, distinguish shared caps from child spending and clarify resume-versus-clear None semantics.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/OpenHands/software-agent-sdk/pull/6257" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/OpenHands/software-agent-sdk/pull/6279" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a> · <a href="https://github.com/OpenHands/software-agent-sdk/pull/6197" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 3</a> · <a href="https://github.com/OpenHands/software-agent-sdk/pull/6259" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 4</a></p><h2 id="_7-openhands-1-34-0-ships-per-conversation-docker-runtime-and-key-rotation-repair" tabindex="-1">7. OpenHands 1.34.0 ships per-conversation Docker runtime and key-rotation repair <a class="header-anchor" href="#_7-openhands-1-34-0-ships-per-conversation-docker-runtime-and-key-rotation-repair" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7. OpenHands 1.34.0 ships per-conversation Docker runtime and key-rotation repair&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：RELEASED</strong></p><p>Version 1.34.0 releases per-conversation Docker runtime and Runtime settings. It includes #18307, previously only confirmed merged, pinning the automation KV secret across session-key rotation. It also passes static-server keys through environment instead of argv, isolates invalid OAuth relay attempts and hardens untrusted Markdown opener/target handling. This confirms the application release notes only; concurrent SDK-main budget/damaged-log changes are not automatically attributed to this version.</p><p>Pending lead: separately verify runtime selection, credential delivery and encryption-key lifetime; environment-based delivery is not a guarantee of secrecy. Interaction: upgrade KV-read regression and binding runtime selections to existing conversations.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/OpenHands/OpenHands/releases/tag/v1.34.0" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a></p><h2 id="_8-openai-agents-python-stops-queued-native-tools-during-failure-cleanup" tabindex="-1">8. OpenAI Agents Python stops queued native tools during failure cleanup <a class="header-anchor" href="#_8-openai-agents-python-stops-queued-native-tools-during-failure-cleanup" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;8. OpenAI Agents Python stops queued native tools during failure cleanup&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：MERGED #5446; RELEASE NOT VERIFIED</strong></p><p>#5446 stops queued native tools from continuing while completed outputs are drained after a selected function-tool failure, including parallel input-guardrail rejection. It cancels active native categories and closes invocation queues while preserving the selected error, bounded finalization of completed outputs, approvals and rejected-input history exclusion. Already-cancelled tasks receive the existing grace then another cancellation; genuine caller cancellation bypasses the grace. Public APIs/persisted state are unchanged.</p><p>Pending lead: admission rejection must constrain queued parallel work; failure cleanup must not create a new execution window. Interaction: in-flight effects and priority between output finalization and cancellation.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/openai/openai-agents-python/pull/5446" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a></p><h2 id="_9-google-adk-removes-rewound-memories-and-avoids-replaying-finished-nodes" tabindex="-1">9. Google ADK removes rewound memories and avoids replaying finished nodes <a class="header-anchor" href="#_9-google-adk-removes-rewound-memories-and-avoids-replaying-finished-nodes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;9. Google ADK removes rewound memories and avoids replaying finished nodes&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：COMMITTED ON MAIN; RELEASE NOT VERIFIED</strong></p><p>Commit 2591397 (#7571) deletes explicitly rewound session.events from SQLite/Firestore memory on add_session_to_memory, while retaining older events simply absent because only recent events were loaded. b8d69f8 (#7753) uses None for sessionless memory so caller-supplied special session IDs cannot replace it. d0c3af5 (#7554) recognizes nodes already completed after partial interrupt responses and fast-forwards their recorded results on later resume rather than rerunning effects.</p><p>Pending lead: distinguish rewound, unloaded and missing data; decide replay using completion facts rather than unresolved interrupts alone. Interaction: propagating memory withdrawal, reserved identifiers and cross-store consistency.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/google/adk-python/commit/2591397bba40f1d0899aca0ea37c162381d0a119" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/google/adk-python/commit/b8d69f8541f74cc690f4adf1d7ea7c4c1d508fa0" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a> · <a href="https://github.com/google/adk-python/commit/d0c3af571faf71252a226bbcb4891cb08374d773" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 3</a></p><h2 id="_10-beads-preserves-creation-commit-order-after-journal-pruning" tabindex="-1">10. Beads preserves creation commit order after journal pruning <a class="header-anchor" href="#_10-beads-preserves-creation-commit-order-after-journal-pruning" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10. Beads preserves creation commit order after journal pruning&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：MERGED #7912; RELEASE NOT VERIFIED</strong></p><p>#7912 stamps ephemeral/no_history wisp-plane rows with arrival_seq in the create journal transaction, using that record&#39;s sequence. After falling behind retention and receiving 410, consumers can rebuild creation order from surviving rows rather than second-resolution created_at. Only the platform writes it; client create/update/import values are ignored. Values compare within one database, gaps are normal, durable beads have none and exports/replication do not propagate meaningful values. Journal-disabled rows may remain unstamped.</p><p>High learning value, medium competitive risk; retain main-radar event-reconstruction monitoring. Pending FCoP lead: preserve order evidence with retained rows without treating it as a global cross-database clock. Interaction: unstamped rows, synthetic creates on import/rename and recovery watermarks.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/gastownhall/beads/pull/7912" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a></p><h2 id="_11-eigent-restores-answerable-questions-and-explicit-memory-change-decisions" tabindex="-1">11. Eigent restores answerable questions and explicit Memory-change decisions <a class="header-anchor" href="#_11-eigent-restores-answerable-questions-and-explicit-memory-change-decisions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;11. Eigent restores answerable questions and explicit Memory-change decisions&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：MERGED #2109/#2119/#2120; RELEASE NOT VERIFIED</strong></p><p>#2119 restores an answer control for questions still pending after window reload instead of an inert replay card. #2120 shows Memory changes with before/after Confirm/Reject, prevents typed text from acting as approval and removes duplicate empty review cards. #2109 uses the original request and this run&#39;s browser evidence to request reconnect, ignores other Sessions and caps evidence lookup at 1.5 seconds. History requests reconnection but grants no browser access. Manual desktop acceptance remains incomplete.</p><p>High learning value and medium-high competitive risk; increase main-radar human-governance/recovery attention. Pending lead: playback must not swallow live human requests; memory modification is not ordinary Q&amp;A. Interaction: approval freshness, tool reauthorization and late lookup results.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/eigent-ai/eigent/pull/2119" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/eigent-ai/eigent/pull/2120" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a> · <a href="https://github.com/eigent-ai/eigent/pull/2109" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 3</a></p><h2 id="_12-crewai-1-15-31-releases-yesterday-s-security-fixes" tabindex="-1">12. CrewAI 1.15.31 releases yesterday&#39;s security fixes <a class="header-anchor" href="#_12-crewai-1-15-31-releases-yesterday-s-security-fixes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;12. CrewAI 1.15.31 releases yesterday&#39;s security fixes&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：RELEASED; STATE ADVANCE FROM MERGED</strong></p><p>The 1.15.31 release explicitly includes output_file interpolation traversal, checkpoint deserialization, SQL-comment bypass and tar-extraction race fixes. It also adds conversational-flow context compaction and drops checkpoint resume state when copying crews. This advances yesterday&#39;s merged/unreleased status, not the date the vulnerabilities arose. Version 1.15.32 primarily changes tests/documentation and is not counted separately.</p><p>Pending lead: there is now a definite release candidate for consumers of these paths; copying a crew must not duplicate execution identity. Interaction: compatibility, post-copy state and old-checkpoint policy.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/crewAIInc/crewAI/releases/tag/1.15.31" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a></p><h2 id="_13-ag2-1-2-2-ships-tool-subscriber-timeouts-and-announces-api-migration" tabindex="-1">13. AG2 1.2.2 ships tool/subscriber timeouts and announces API migration <a class="header-anchor" href="#_13-ag2-1-2-2-ships-tool-subscriber-timeouts-and-announces-api-migration" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;13. AG2 1.2.2 ships tool/subscriber timeouts and announces API migration&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：RELEASED</strong></p><p>Version 1.2.2 releases yesterday&#39;s tool-timeout and failure-attribution fixes, extending timeouts to stream subscribers, observers, LoopDetector and TokenMonitor callbacks. Timed-out synchronous workers can still produce effects. OpenAIConfig currently keeps Chat Completions behavior with deprecation warnings and is scheduled to become Responses in 1.4.0; OpenAICompletionsConfig preserves the old API. This version does not automatically migrate it. Anthropic SDK 2.x support also introduces sampling-parameter deprecation/drop behavior.</p><p>Pending lead: event consumers can block execution, and API class names do not ensure stable semantics. Interaction: event loss/retry after callback timeouts and validation of API-specific option migration.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/ag2ai/ag2/releases/tag/v1.2.2" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a></p><h2 id="_14-langfuse-4-71-0-releases-government-fips-licensing-changes" tabindex="-1">14. Langfuse 4.71.0 releases Government/FIPS licensing changes <a class="header-anchor" href="#_14-langfuse-4-71-0-releases-government-fips-licensing-changes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;14. Langfuse 4.71.0 releases Government/FIPS licensing changes&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：RELEASED; STATE ADVANCE FROM MERGED</strong></p><p>Version 4.71.0 explicitly includes #19316: self-hosted FIPS now requires lf_gov_ Government keys; enterprise keys no longer suffice. Government disables telemetry and Cloud is unchanged. Yesterday this was explicitly not attributed to 4.70.1; it is now released. Gateway generations also record model, key owner, bytes and upstream errors. Version 3.225.15 backports organization checks, legacy webhook-header encryption and renewed secret requirements after destination changes.</p><p>Pending lead: compliance-license gates are now a real upgrade boundary rather than a future PR risk; FIPS users should verify entitlement before upgrading. Interaction: migration windows, offline-key diagnostics and reauthorization scope after destination changes.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/langfuse/langfuse/releases/tag/v4.71.0" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/langfuse/langfuse/releases/tag/v3.225.15" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a></p><h2 id="_15-mcp-merges-its-release-process-proposal-acceptance-predates-this-run" tabindex="-1">15. MCP merges its release-process proposal; acceptance predates this run <a class="header-anchor" href="#_15-mcp-merges-its-release-process-proposal-acceptance-predates-this-run" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;15. MCP merges its release-process proposal; acceptance predates this run&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：MERGED #3398; SEP STATUS ACCEPTED SINCE 2026-10-14</strong></p><p>#3398 merges SEP-3398 in this window, but its Accepted status dates to October 14. The process specifies releases generally on the last Tuesday of March/September, overlapping eight-month cycles, rotating release managers and shared SEP review/freeze/SDK milestones. The transition proposes moving the December 2026 target to late March 2027. This is a Process SEP, not a wire API or evidence that any proposal is Final/implemented by SDKs.</p><p>Pending lead: plan compatibility using separate maturity states for specifications, extensions and SDKs. Interaction: emergency security approval, deferred extensions and SDK milestones in compatibility testing.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/pull/3398" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/blob/main/seps/3398-release-cycle-updates.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a></p><h2 id="_16-orkas-v2026-10-27-expands-byok-media-and-custom-model-protocols" tabindex="-1">16. Orkas v2026.10.27 expands BYOK media and custom model protocols <a class="header-anchor" href="#_16-orkas-v2026-10-27-expands-byok-media-and-custom-model-protocols" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;16. Orkas v2026.10.27 expands BYOK media and custom model protocols&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：RELEASED</strong></p><p>The release adds user-provider keys for public image/video models and custom models over OpenAI Responses, Chat Completions or Anthropic protocols, with capacity validation and credential recovery. It separates local app creation/preview from publication/invitations and improves automation/to-do access and interrupted voice drafts. The official download page lists Apple Silicon/Intel DMGs and Windows x64 EXE and claims signed Windows installers. No installer/signature was locally verified, and these formats are not claimed newly introduced today.</p><p>Medium-high learning value and competitive risk; retain the main radar with more BYOK/delivery attention. Pending lead: separate local creation, external publication and provider-credential authority. Interaction: protocol capability detection, capacity checks and task recovery after key rotation.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/Orkas-AI/Orkas/releases/tag/v2026.10.27" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://orkas.ai/download/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a></p><h2 id="_17-gemini-cli-0-66-0-ships-private-mcp-oauth-opt-in-and-active-install-verification" tabindex="-1">17. Gemini CLI 0.66.0 ships private MCP OAuth opt-in and active-install verification <a class="header-anchor" href="#_17-gemini-cli-0-66-0-ships-private-mcp-oauth-opt-in-and-active-install-verification" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;17. Gemini CLI 0.66.0 ships private MCP OAuth opt-in and active-install verification&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：RELEASED; REFERENCED PRS MERGED EARLIER</strong></p><p>Version 0.66.0 is released in this window. #29844, merged October 20, enables enterprise-private MCP OAuth endpoints only with per-server oauth.allowPrivateNetwork opt-in, preserving default boundaries rather than allowing all special addresses. #29757, merged October 16, checks the active binary&#39;s package version after a successful updater exit to detect PATH shadowing; indeterminate package metadata retains compatibility fallback, not strict fail-closed verification. The new event is release availability, not the old merge dates.</p><p>Pending lead: scope private-network authority per server and verify the actually executed installation after updates. Interaction: DNS re-resolution, redirect chains and explicit uncertainty when metadata cannot be read.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/google-gemini/gemini-cli/releases/tag/v0.66.0" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/google-gemini/gemini-cli/pull/29844" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a> · <a href="https://github.com/google-gemini/gemini-cli/pull/29757" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 3</a></p><h2 id="_18-orca-advances-daemon-generation-because-app-updates-do-not-replace-live-daemons" tabindex="-1">18. Orca advances daemon generation because app updates do not replace live daemons <a class="header-anchor" href="#_18-orca-advances-daemon-generation-because-app-updates-do-not-replace-live-daemons" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;18. Orca advances daemon generation because app updates do not replace live daemons&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status：MERGED #34627/#34603; STABLE RELEASE NOT VERIFIED</strong></p><p>#34627 advances daemon protocol 52 to 53 so new terminals get daemon-side fixes while old v52 terminals remain attachable. Both 1.4.241/242 use v52; v53 previously existed only in an ad hoc build, not a stable release. #34603 makes POSIX force-kill/resize ps and /proc queries asynchronous to avoid freezing all terminals, accounts for root exit/PID reuse and preserves different synchronous ConPTY teardown rules on Windows.</p><p>High learning value and medium-high competitive risk; keep it prominent in the second circle with more upgrade/worker-lifecycle attention. Pending lead: application, host, worker versions and live processes are distinct release facts. Interaction: old/new daemon coexistence, session migration and rollback compatibility.</p><p>Primary sources：<a href="https://github.com/stablyai/orca/pull/34627" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 1</a> · <a href="https://github.com/stablyai/orca/pull/34603" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Evidence 2</a></p><h2 id="cross-project-synthesis-and-disposition" tabindex="-1">Cross-project synthesis and disposition <a class="header-anchor" href="#cross-project-synthesis-and-disposition" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Cross-project synthesis and disposition&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Execution identity and recovery authority:</strong> Paperclip&#39;s fenced adoption, Paradev&#39;s viewer credentials and Eigent&#39;s non-authorizing browser history constrain identity/permission drift during recovery.</li><li><strong>Availability is not evidence completeness:</strong> readable damaged OpenHands logs retain gaps, Paperclip HTTP readiness is not adoption completion, and Beads ordering stamps are database-scoped.</li><li><strong>Stopping/withdrawal must constrain later state:</strong> Agents SDK closes queues, ADK removes rewound memories and Codex prevents post-interrupt wakes.</li><li><strong>Verify released and running versions separately:</strong> CrewAI/AG2/Langfuse advance from merges to releases; Orca app updates do not necessarily replace daemons; a successful Gemini updater process does not prove the active binary changed.</li></ul><p>Disposition is collection, screening and reporting only. Upstream experiments are not local experiments. Local experiments: 0; articles: 0; formal development reviews: 0; external comments/messages: 0. Technical interaction topics are recorded without creating development assignments.</p><h2 id="dual-source-verification-coverage-and-gaps" tabindex="-1">Dual-source verification, coverage and gaps <a class="header-anchor" href="#dual-source-verification-coverage-and-gaps" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Dual-source verification, coverage and gaps&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The connected GitHub tool read latest-main Registry first: 47 researchers, blob 8d97df65269aa2b4accd1366d1b71319f99c6efd. The six governance/verification researchers retain the documented relationship boundaries; no separate meaningful repository change was confirmed for them. Mansura&#39;s bulbasaur-skill-cli is not presented as AGENTSAFE&#39;s official implementation; papers, people and repositories are not one-to-one.</p><p>Coverage spans 88 targets including Registry sources, explicit technical supplements and 32 competitors. Checks cover releases, incremental commits and updated PRs/issues, with primary PR/document/code reads for selected changes. There are 470 commit records across 29 changed targets; 58 accessible zero-increment targets receive separate default-head reads. Discussions, every document and every license were not exhaustively inspected. Superset remains the monitored name, accessed through verified ParadevAI/Paradev with the same repository ID 1080370450.</p><p>Gaps: Codex, Claude Code and Orca PR/issue feeds remain partial after 12 pages and retain earlier pending windows. TSA-AI-Workforce returns actual 404s on all three feeds; deletion/private status is not inferred. Codex&#39;s large release listing failed and latest-three/latest-stable reads recovered bounded coverage. Most other release checks cover the latest ten. Dynamic pagination retains record counts and unique_count without claiming full history. Only completed successful queries advance their source watermarks; no global full-coverage watermark exists.</p><p>The second source type is recorded separately: 12 grouped public-social/author-discussion searches for the current 47 people, plus direct opens of Cursor&#39;s official changelog, Audit Commons, Paradev&#39;s changelog and Orkas&#39;s download page. No reliably dated, separately meaningful personal post was established in this window; this does not exhaust X timelines. Cursor&#39;s currently visible October 20 and Audit Commons&#39; October 24 material are historical leads and not repeated as new. Orkas supports delivery-format claims; signatures/installers were not locally verified. GitHub primary events independently support engineering facts; two GitHub links are not two source types.</p><p>Zhao-specific checks cover existing auditable/GRADE/CatchBench sources and new agent-config, agent-style and awesome-auditable-ai commits. Visible additions mainly back up reviewed material, logos, pack configuration and temporary-file conventions; no new method, benchmark result or release was established. Backup commits are not counted for volume. Aegis only shows prose polish here, so yesterday&#39;s adaptive experiment is not repeated.</p><h2 id="github-archive-and-website-delivery" tabindex="-1">GitHub archive and website delivery <a class="header-anchor" href="#github-archive-and-website-delivery" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;GitHub archive and website delivery&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Both languages retain the same 18 groups, states, 37 evidence links and judgments. The connected GitHub tool updates only the two dated reports and original indexes, preserving structure/fixed entry points. The existing site implementation and non-force gh-pages publication are used. Latest-main/publication-branch concurrency checks, four-file readback, matching Pages success and bilingual live click acceptance are stored in a local receipt; this body does not pre-claim completion. Original layout, public snapshots, other published work and translation caches are preserved. Research Runtime lifecycle facts, article candidates and scheduler state are untouched.</p>',104)])])}const g=a(s,[["render",i]]);export{h as __pageData,g as default};
